00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.394
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:25.870
Contrary to what other
web devs might tell you,

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.240
CORS is a security
feature of browsers

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.780
and not just a frustration
dream come to life.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:35.110
First, I'll give an
overview of what CORS is.

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:38.260
Then I'll address how to
implement CORS for Google Cloud

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.210
Storage objects.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:43.330
Let's get to the basics
of understanding CORS.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:48.430
First of all, CORS is short for
Cross-Origin Resource Sharing.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:50.560
To understand what
this means, it really

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.000
helps to first understand
same origin resource sharing.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.210
The same origin policy
is a security policy

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:01.240
enforced on web browsers
to prevent interactions

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.740
between resources from
different origins, like domains.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.650
I like to think of it as
preventing a bait and switch.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.400
If I go to a website,
say, cat.pics,

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.510
I expect to interact with
resources of cat.pics.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:20.560
But what if cat.pics,
instead of showing photos

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.310
of adorable cats, downloaded
malicious software

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.460
from some other website?

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.080
The same origin policy
looks at the calls that

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.690
are coming from a
website and ensures

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:36.490
they come from, you guessed
it, the same origin.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:39.440
So the same origin
policy is a good thing.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.420
It protects users.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.950
However, it can also prevent
legitimate interactions

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.020
between known origins.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:49.900
For example, a script
on a page hosted

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.380
on App Engine at
example.appspot.com

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:57.910
might need to use resources
stored in a Cloud Storage

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:02.530
bucket at
example.storage.googleapis.com.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:04.810
These are two different
origins from the perspective

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:05.750
of the browser.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:09.130
Thanks to that trusty same
origin policy, the browser

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:12.820
won't allow a script
from example.appspot.com

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:17.680
to fetch resources from
example.storage.googleapis.com.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.040
You've probably experienced
this as a CORS error.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.150
Admittedly, not ideal,
but take a deep breath.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.880
What does this mean
and how do we fix it?

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.510
The Cross-Origin
Resource Sharing spec,

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:34.900
also known as CORS, was
developed by the Worldwide Web

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.230
Consortium to get around the
limitations of same origin

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:39.550
policy.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:42.640
Cloud storage supports CORS
by allowing you to configure

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.750
your buckets to support it.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.440
So in our example
case, you can configure

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.140
the
example.storage.googleapis.com

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.600
bucket so that a
browser can share

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:57.310
its resources with the scripts
from example.appspot.com.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.410
And there you go,
no more CORS error.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.500
Now that you know a
bit more about CORS,

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.600
you're ready to get configuring.

